! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1268 TEST 2 -- the deck's own test that a
! LOAD CLOSE TO A STORE reads the new value, in all three memories.
! PDF p.111 (printed p.108), octal 024363.40-024375.40, transcribed
! VERBATIM at the listing's own addresses, checked bit-exactly by
! verify_ax5b_1683_listing.py AND RUN ON THE MODEL.
!
! WHAT ROUTINE 1268 ACTUALLY IS, because this project had it wrong.
! docs/NEXT.md item 2 called 1268 "the only known deck test" of the DF
! and IF ADDRESS-MONITORING INDICATORS, and said to read it before
! designing them.  Read first-hand 2026-09-22, it is nothing of the
! kind.  The routine's own header (PDF p.110):
!
!     ----1268---IF AND DF CLOSE TO DS.
!
! (CORRECTED 2026-09-24: this line read "----1268----" with FOUR
! trailing dashes.  The dash band was measured numerically at 500 dpi
! rather than eyeballed -- in p.110's dash rows the ink forms runs of
! 44 and 148 px before "1268" and 48 and 96 px after, against a ~48 px
! character pitch fixed by the "1268" stems, giving FOUR dashes before
! and THREE after.  Every other banner transcribed in this directory --
! 1238, 1244, 1246, 1250, 1258, 1262 -- is 4-and-3, so this file was
! the sole outlier.  Nothing depends on it; banners carry no machine
! code.  Recorded because a dash count is exactly the kind of detail
! that gets copied forward unchecked.)
!     -TEST 1 CHECKS IF CLOSE TO DS IN ALL 3 MEMORIES.
!     -TEST 2 CHECKS DF CLOSE TO DS IN ALL 3 MEMORIES.
!
! and its error messages settle what is being checked:
!     test 1   "EXECUTED OLD INSTRUCTION FROM EXT."
!     test 2   "LOADED OLD DATA FROM EXT MEM."
!
! Compare routine 1260's indicator blocks, which say "UNABLE TO TURN
! AD ON." when an INDICATOR fails to set.  These say the wrong VALUE
! arrived.  DF and IF here are the DATA FETCH and INSTRUCTION FETCH
! paths, and "close to DS" means temporally close to a DATA STORE:
! 1268 is a LOOK-AHEAD STORE-TO-FETCH / STORE-TO-LOAD FORWARDING test.
! Nothing in it writes a mask, sets a boundary register, branches on
! DF or IF, or touches protected storage -- 168K3 is ordinary storage
! at 024411.00.
!
! docs/archival_inventory.md contained BOTH readings, twenty lines
! apart; the one that called 1268 "Look-Ahead store-to-fetch/
! store-to-load forwarding" was right.  So DF and IF, the indicators,
! have NO deck oracle in this document -- and 1268 turns out to be a
! deck oracle for something the model ALREADY implements, where the
! project had only hand-written tests (store_close_to_fetch.strap,
! ebox_register_close_to_fetch.strap).  That is a better outcome than
! item 2 expected, and a worse premise.
!
! WHAT THE BLOCK DOES, three times over.  Zero the destination, load
! X1 with an index word of ones, zero X2, STORE X1 into the
! destination, immediately LOAD it back, and branch to the error
! routine if the loaded value is zero -- i.e. if the load read the
! stale word instead of the one just stored.  The three destinations
! are the three memories the routine index names -- though on THIS
! model two of them are special-register words rather than core, so
! the deck's "all 3 memories" does not mean three distinct paths
! here:
!
!     168K3   external memory   (a scratch word at 024411.00)
!     $X4     index storage     (word 20)
!     $R      internal memory   (word 9, the accumulator's right half)
!
! BXVZ is what observes it: the index result indicators describe the
! index an operation just wrote (Ref. Manual p.23), so the LX sets
! XVZ from the value it loaded.  Stale read -> value zero -> XVZ ->
! branch to SERS.
!
! WHY CASE 2C LOADS INTO $X1 AND NOT $X2.  The listing prints
! "LX,$X1,$R" at 024374.40 ("11.02 10"; .02 is X1, .04 is X2 in the
! rows above), so its "Z,$X2" is vestigial.  It still works, because
! XVZ describes whichever index the LX wrote.  Transcribed as printed.
!
! THIS ONE RUNS, unlike the four routine-1260 blocks -- but be exact
! about what that buys, because it is less than it looks.
!
! IT PASSES, AND IT DOES NOT WITNESS FORWARDING ON THIS MODEL.
! Measured 2026-09-22.  StretchStorage.bsv holds the ordering rule
! this block is supposed to exercise:
!
!     // Every read waits for queued stores to leave the queue:
!     // store->load ordering for the operand channel ...
!     if (!busUsed && !issued && !storeQ.notEmpty) begin
!
! Removing "&& !storeQ.notEmpty" leaves THIS BLOCK GREEN.  It also
! leaves store_close_to_fetch.strap green.  What it breaks is
! ebox_register_close_to_fetch.strap, which fails two checks
! (SEN = 0x0700e00000000000 for 0, halt 1416 for 14343).  So the guard
! IS witnessed -- by the hand-written E-box test, not by the deck's.
!
! WHY THE DECK'S OWN TEST IS THE WEAKER ONE HERE.  The deck spaces its
! store and load one instruction apart, which was tight enough for the
! real machine's look-ahead.  This model's pipeline is shallower, so
! the store queue has drained by the time the load issues and the
! hazard never appears.  The E-box test manufactures a tighter overlap
! deliberately.  A 1961 diagnostic being too gentle for a 2026 model is
! the reverse of the usual direction, and worth knowing before reaching
! for a deck as an oracle.
!
! SO THE TWO STANDARDS HERE ARE NOT EQUAL.  The ENCODING check
! (verify_ax5b_1683_listing.py) is the discriminating one and has its
! own injections.  The RUN is a regression guard: it pins that the
! deck's own logic still completes, and it becomes a real oracle when
! the look-ahead's LAAR forwarding replaces the ordering rule --
! StretchStorage.bsv's comment says that is stage 3.
!
! SUBSTITUTIONS: three, all named.
!   1. Numeric labels are prefixed "L" (this assembler reads a leading
!      digit as an address): 1683, 1000, 168K3.
!   2. SERS, the deck's error-report routine, is a "B,$" HANG at its
!      own printed address 001304.00 -- so a failure halts there and
!      the testbench's PC check names which case failed.  The real
!      SERS is not transcribed.
!   3. A "BEW,$" exit is placed at 024376.00, where the listing's own
!      "B,$+1.0" continues into the rest of the routine.
! Everything else -- including L1000's all-ones index word at
! 024503.00 and L168K3's zero word at 024411.00 -- is at the address
! the listing prints for it.

L1000   SYN, (8)24503.0            ! "INDX WD. OF ONES." (p.114)
L168K3  SYN, (8)24411.0            ! the external-memory scratch word
SEN     SYN, (8)1310.0             ! the SIC error-entry link
SERS    SYN, (8)1304.0             ! error routine -- a hang here

        SLC, (8)24363.40
L1683   Z,   L168K3                ! -TEST 2A,DF CLOSE TO DS IN EXT MEM.
        LX,  $X1, L1000
        Z,   $X2
        SX,  $X1, L168K3           !  store...
        LX,  $X2, L168K3           !  ...and load it straight back
        SIC, SEN
        BXVZ, SERS                 ! -LOADED OLD DATA FROM EXT MEM.
!
        Z,   $X4                   !  index storage
        LX,  $X1, L1000
        Z,   $X2
        SX,  $X1, $X4
        LX,  $X2, $X4
        SIC, SEN
        BXVZ, SERS                 ! -LOADED OLD DATA FROM IX STG.
!
        Z,   $R                    !  internal memory
        LX,  $X1, L1000
        Z,   $X2
        SX,  $X1, $R
        LX,  $X1, $R               !  $X1, as printed -- see the header
        SIC, SEN
        BXVZ, SERS                 ! -LOADED OLD DATA FROM INT MEM.
!
! ---- substitution 3: the exit ---------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)24376.0
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the block's own constants, at their printed addresses ----------
        SLC, (8)24411.0
K3      XW, 0, 0, 0                ! 168K3
        SLC, (8)24503.0
ONES    DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777   ! "INDX WD. OF ONES."
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SENW    DD(BU,64), 0               ! SIC's target
!
! ---- substitution 2: SERS is a hang ---------------------------------
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERSH   B, $
        END, L1683
